# HR Employee Attrition Analysis

[![Python](https://img.shields.io/badge/Python-3.11+-blue.svg)](https://www.python.org/downloads/)
[![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-green.svg)](LICENSE)
[![Code Style](https://img.shields.io/badge/Code%20Style-Ruff-black.svg)](https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff)
[![Type Check](https://img.shields.io/badge/Type%20Check-mypy-blue.svg)](https://mypy-lang.org/)
[![Tests](https://img.shields.io/badge/Tests-pytest-green.svg)](https://pytest.org/)
[![CI](https://github.com/Yassi0022/HR-Attrition-Analysis/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Yassi0022/HR-Attrition-Analysis/actions/workflows/ci.yml)

End-to-end machine learning pipeline for predicting employee attrition using the **IBM HR Analytics Employee Attrition Dataset**. This project demonstrates professional data science practices: clean modular code, proper train/test splits (no data leakage), reproducible preprocessing pipelines, comprehensive testing, and CI/CD.

---

## 📊 Project Overview

This project analyzes factors contributing to employee turnover and builds predictive models to identify at-risk employees. The pipeline includes:

- **Exploratory Data Analysis** — Statistical summaries, attrition rates by department
- **Visualizations** — Age distribution, income analysis, attrition by categorical features
- **Correlation Analysis** — Feature correlation heatmaps and strong correlation detection
- **Model Training** — Logistic Regression & Random Forest with proper preprocessing
- **Model Evaluation** — Confusion matrices, ROC curves, feature importance

---

## 🏗️ Architecture

```
src/attrition/
├── __init__.py           # Package exports
├── cli.py                # Typer CLI entry points
├── config.py             # Configuration constants (paths, columns, params)
├── utils.py              # Shared utilities (I/O, plotting, model persistence)
├── preprocessing.py      # Preprocessing pipeline (ColumnTransformer, no leakage)
├── exploration.py        # EDA + attrition by department
├── visuals.py            # All visualization functions
├── correlations.py       # Correlation matrix & heatmap
└── model.py              # Model training, evaluation, feature importance
```

**Key Design Decisions:**

| Concern | Solution |
|---------|----------|
| Data Leakage | Train/test split **before** preprocessing; `StandardScaler` fit only on training data |
| Categorical Encoding | `ColumnTransformer` + `OneHotEncoder(drop='first')` — single fitted transformer |
| Reproducibility | Fixed `RANDOM_STATE=42`, pinned dependencies in `requirements.txt` |
| Modularity | Each analysis step is a reusable function with type hints |
| Testing | Unit tests for all modules; CI runs lint, type-check, tests on every push |

---

## 🚀 Quickstart

### Prerequisites

- Python 3.11+
- Git

### Installation

```bash
# Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/Yassi0022/HR-Attrition-Analysis.git
cd HR-Attrition-Analysis

# Create virtual environment
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate  # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate

# Install package in development mode with dev dependencies
pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

### Run the Complete Pipeline

```bash
# Run everything: exploration, visualizations, correlations, model training
attrition all

# Or run individual steps
attrition explore      # EDA + attrition by department
attrition visualize    # All plots (age, income, attrition by category)
attrition correlate    # Correlation heatmap + strong correlations
attrition train        # Train Logistic Regression + Random Forest
attrition feature_importance  # RF feature importance on full dataset
```

### Outputs

All outputs are saved to organized directories:

```
img/           # Visualizations (PNG, 150 DPI)
models/        # Trained models (.joblib) + metrics (JSON)
reports/       # CSV reports (feature importance, etc.)
```

